From the k72/k77/k80 review (2026-11-21). (1) After a k70 give-up the log says "retry with ue_start" (cmd/launcher/lifecycle.go:141); with cleanOnRestart on, that manual start empties Saved/Crashes, so an agent following the advice loses the crash-loop dumps. Neither the line nor the ue_start description warns. (2) killCrashReporter runs on the automatic restart too, about one restart delay after the crash, and may cut off a reporter still writing the dump (older behaviour, unverified). Done: decide whether a manual start right after a give-up keeps Saved/Crashes (or the advice says so), and whether the automatic restart waits for or skips the reporter kill; tests on Linux. Lane: unreagent-worker.

2026-11-21 09:21 Worker: (1) Main agent's decision: behaviour over advice. supervisor.Start (PreStart's argument, replacing the automatic bool) gains AfterGiveUp: true for the first start after the service failed and was not restarted (fast-fail give-up, used-up maxRestarts, a policy that does not restart, a start that cannot be retried). It is set where OnExit hears of the failure and used up by the next start. The launcher's followsCrash is Automatic || AfterGiveUp; cleanRecovery keeps Saved/Crashes on it and logs 'Recovery: Saved/Crashes kept (first start since the editor failed and was not restarted — the crash dumps stay for diagnosis)'. The give-up line names the folder: '… Any crash dumps are in <project>/Saved/Crashes' plus, with cleanOnRestart, ' — a retry keeps them, a new launcher run empties them (unreal.cleanOnRestart)' (MCP on) or ' — the next launcher run empties them (unreal.cleanOnRestart)' (MCP off: the launcher stops, and no state carries over to the next run, so here only advice is possible). Red first: TestRunCleanOnRestartKeepsCrashesOnTheFirstStartAfterAGiveUp (crash loop, give-up, ue_start over MCP: the editor found Saved/Crashes empty), TestPreStartSaysWhetherTheStartFollowsAGiveUp, TestEditorGiveUpLineSaysWhereTheDumpsAre. (2) Main agent's decision: do not cut off a reporter that may still be writing. My choice: skip the kill on a start that follows a crash, not a bounded wait. Skipping cannot hold the restart up; a wait needs a bound and a way to tell when the reporter is done, and still kills a slow reporter at the bound. The editor's PreStart moved out of run() into editorPreStart (lifecycle.go). Also my choice: the first start after a give-up skips the kill too (one followsCrash rule), so a stuck reporter is gone by the second start somebody asks for. Red first: TestEditorPreStartSparesTheCrashReporterAfterACrash. Finding, unverified (Windows/UE): a CrashReportClient the editor spawns is in the editor's Job Object (no BREAKAWAY_OK), and